# Rule: SwiftData Change Tracking

Sources

https://developer.apple.com/documentation/swiftdata/fetching-and-filtering-time-based-model-changes
https://developer.apple.com/documentation/swiftdata/historydescriptor
https://developer.apple.com/documentation/swiftdata/historychange
https://developer.apple.com/documentation/swiftdata/historyproviding
https://developer.apple.com/documentation/swiftdata/defaultstore

Scope

Applies to:

- history transaction retrieval and filtering
- durable token management and incremental processing
- model-change interpretation and re-fetch strategies
- history cleanup and bounded retention policies

Intent

Process persisted changes as ordered transactions with durable checkpoints to
support reliable sync and cross-process consistency.

Core Principle

Track persisted evolution as ordered history transactions; process incrementally
using durable tokens.

Implementation Rules

- Fetch history with `HistoryDescriptor` scoped by token/author/predicate.
- Persist the most recent processed token after successful processing.
- Filter each transaction's changes to only model types/attributes relevant to current workflow.
- Use changed model identifiers to refetch materialized models when needed.
- Configure attribute preservation for delete tombstone scenarios that require stable post-delete identifiers.
- Delete stale history in bounded windows after downstream processing succeeds.

Canonical Patterns

```swift
var descriptor = HistoryDescriptor<History.DefaultTransaction>()
descriptor.predicate = #Predicate { $0.token > token }
let txns = try context.fetchHistory(descriptor)
```

```swift
try context.deleteHistory(descriptorForExpiredTransactions)
```

Anti-Patterns

- Reprocessing full history without token checkpoints.
- Treating all transaction changes as relevant to every feature.
- Deleting history before consumers finish processing.
- Assuming deleted model values remain available without preserve-on-deletion setup.

Checklist

- Is token-based incremental processing in place?
- Are transactions filtered by relevance?
- Are changed identifiers resolved safely before action?
- Is history cleanup policy defined and safe?

Architectural Implications

- History processing is an operational data pipeline, not a UI convenience API.
- Token checkpoints are durability boundaries and must be persisted reliably.
- Change relevance filtering prevents accidental cross-feature coupling.

Advanced Notes

- Transactions are heterogeneous; do not assume one transaction maps to one feature concern.
- Preserve-on-deletion attributes are necessary for stable post-delete correlation use-cases.
- Cleanup timing impacts recoverability and storage footprint; define retention policy explicitly.

Integration With Architecture

- Keep history readers in sync/integration layers, not in presentation views.
- Route filtered change events into domain-specific handlers.
- Coordinate token persistence with app lifecycle and background task completion semantics.

Enforcement Summary

Always prefer:

- token-based incremental history processing
- strict relevance filtering
- identifier-based refetch for changed entities
- explicit stale-history cleanup policy

Never allow:

- replaying full history by default
- feature logic triggered by unrelated transaction noise
- premature history deletion before downstream consumers finish
- reliance on deleted values without tombstone strategy
